WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.318
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.657 --> 00:00:07.990
NATALIE PIUCCO: Hi, I'm Natalie.

00:00:07.990 --> 00:00:10.750
And we are in the
Intelligence Neighborhood

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.490
in the Sounds and Models demo.

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.100
And I'm joined here by Yufeng.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.540
Yufeng, tell us a little
bit about what you do

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.840
and the awesome demo
that you've created.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:22.300
YUFENG GUO: Here, we have
station one gathering data.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.682
And we have this kind of
instrument, if you will.

00:00:24.682 --> 00:00:26.140
But it's actually
five instruments.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.348
And so each string represents
a different instrument,

00:00:28.348 --> 00:00:32.049
and we can pluck our strings
to make different sounds.

00:00:32.049 --> 00:00:34.540
And what we're going
to do is use this

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:36.790
as our kind of data source.

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:40.570
The sounds are going to
then go into Dataflow

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.550
and get converted
into spectrograms.

00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:45.580
They're going to get moved
into the frequency space.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:49.040
And those images can then
be used to train our models.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.910
The hope is that these images
will be distinct enough

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:53.907
to be able to--

00:00:53.907 --> 00:00:55.240
NATALIE PIUCCO: Represent each--

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:55.830
YUFENG GUO: Exactly.

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:57.330
That we can tell
them apart, that we

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.650
can distinguish between
instrument one, two, three,

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:00.445
four, and five.

00:01:00.445 --> 00:01:01.320
NATALIE PIUCCO: Cool.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.980
OK, so we've got
our training data.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.220
Now it's time to start
training the model.

00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:09.610
YUFENG GUO: So with
AutoML, we can train models

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:10.775
using the data that we had.

00:01:10.775 --> 00:01:12.650
But then we don't have
to do any actual work.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:15.243
So we can see here that
the data is coming in.

00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:17.410
And you can see, those are
some of the spectrograms.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.230
Those are the real data that
attendees have generated

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.293
over the course of today.

00:01:21.293 --> 00:01:23.710
NATALIE PIUCCO: And they're
the images of the sound waves.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:24.190
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:25.315
These are the spectrograms.

00:01:25.315 --> 00:01:28.260
And then when you train a model,
it just does the training.

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:30.580
There's not much kind
of work you have to do.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.080
And when it finishes
training, you

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:33.940
can have some
evaluation data that

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:37.055
shows you the metrics around
how well the model trained

00:01:37.055 --> 00:01:37.680
and how it did.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:38.597
NATALIE PIUCCO: Right.

00:01:38.597 --> 00:01:41.592
So if you are new to
ML, that's a great space

00:01:41.592 --> 00:01:42.550
to get started, AutoML.

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:43.300
YUFENG GUO: Right.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:45.465
It's an easy tool
to get going with.

00:01:45.465 --> 00:01:46.840
And if you have
some data and you

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.120
want to throw it on ML Vision,
it'll take care of it for you.

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.520
And you can go do something
else with your time.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.670
So we have this
board here where you

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:58.060
can move these kind of sliders
around to different positions.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:02:01.390
And they map to different
values on the screen, which

00:02:01.390 --> 00:02:05.290
change the values of
type of parameters

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:09.320
of the actual models, things
like kernel width and height,

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:12.370
the layer size, the
number of strides,

00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:14.650
as well as the kind
of number of layers

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.650
you have [INAUDIBLE] have
to change the amount of code

00:02:17.650 --> 00:02:18.580
on the screen.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.250
Once we're happy with the
set of settings that we have,

00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:24.220
we can go ahead and
push the button here

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:25.750
and start a training job.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.940
And doing that,
it will assign us

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.390
a name, something
fun like spinto-cap,

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:34.660
and shoot that off
to Kubeflow, where we

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.040
have a pipeline to do training.

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:37.415
And when it finishes
training, it

00:02:37.415 --> 00:02:40.570
will deploy it on the Cloud AI
Platform Prediction Service.

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:41.570
NATALIE PIUCCO: Awesome.

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:44.108
So time to move to stage
three of training our model.

00:02:44.108 --> 00:02:45.150
YUFENG GUO: That's right.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.230
Speaking of prediction.

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.313
NATALIE PIUCCO: All right.

00:02:47.313 --> 00:02:50.470
So we're at the final
stage now, and that's

00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:51.933
to predict the notes.

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:52.600
YUFENG GUO: Yes.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:54.670
And so we're going to
try to pull up the model

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:56.860
that we've been
training, spinto-cap.

00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:57.610
It's there for us.

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.978
So you can turn the
crank as a selector,

00:02:59.978 --> 00:03:01.770
and then press the red
button to select it.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:02.530
NATALIE PIUCCO: Select.

00:03:02.530 --> 00:03:05.240
YUFENG GUO: So this is kind of
like a customizable music box.

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.860
And we can choose which notes
to play on which instrument.

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.180
NATALIE PIUCCO: More xylophone.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.888
YUFENG GUO: You can
hit the button again.

00:03:11.888 --> 00:03:15.885
So in this countdown, then we'll
be able to turn this crank now.

00:03:15.885 --> 00:03:17.710
And so these are the
notes that we set up.

00:03:17.710 --> 00:03:21.810
And we're going to send those
notes off to the Cloud AI

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:23.650
Platform Prediction Service.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:25.150
Now how did we do, right?

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:26.530
Was our model any good?

00:03:26.530 --> 00:03:29.390
We chose those parameters
kind of randomly.

00:03:29.390 --> 00:03:31.240
And so it might be good.

00:03:31.240 --> 00:03:32.820
It might not be so good.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:33.840
We'll see.

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:34.850
It got some of them.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.058
It seemed like it might have
actually missed a bunch.

00:03:37.058 --> 00:03:37.560
It did.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.750
And so, you know, this is
part of the machine learning

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:41.580
workflow.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:43.663
In this particular case,
a pretty abysmal outcome.

00:03:43.663 --> 00:03:45.038
NATALIE PIUCCO:
That's all right.

00:03:45.038 --> 00:03:47.440
Because that's all part of
machine learning, right?

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:48.870
It's all part of the process.

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:51.740
So I think this is a perfect
demonstration to show that.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:55.273
So Yufeng, thank you so
much for this demonstration.

00:03:55.273 --> 00:03:57.190
I think it was very clear
in kind of the three

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:00.800
stages of what to expect when
training machine learning

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:01.300
models.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:02.590
So thank you so much.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:03.590
YUFENG GUO: My pleasure.

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:04.870
Thanks so much, Natalie.

00:04:04.870 --> 00:04:06.220
NATALIE PIUCCO: And back to you.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:09.570
[MUSIC PLAYING]

